t the exact divergence\n  (inputs, C bits, Rust bits) in your final message.\n- Do not use any external crate other than libloading (already wired through the\n  harness). No `rand`, no `proptest`.\n\nHARNESS API (tests/harness/mod.rs), used as `mod harness; use harness::*;`:\n  both::<F>(name) -> (F, F)          // (C fn ptr, Rust fn ptr) from the two .so\n  both_w::<F>(name) -> (F, F)        // for the `w_*` hooks (libcwrap.so vs Rust)\n  cdata::<T>(name) -> *mut T ; rdata::<T>(name) -> *mut T   // data symbols\n  assert_f32(ctx,c,r) assert_f64 assert_vec(ctx,&[f32],&[f32]) assert_int(ctx,c,r)\n  check_f32(ctx,&inputs,c,r)  check_vec(ctx,&inputs,&c,&r)\n  INTERESTING: &[f32]  // 0,-0,1,-1,.5,90,180,270,360,99999,denormals,MAX,inf,-inf,NaN...\n  Rng::new(seed) with: next_u32, next_u64, below(n), bool(), f32_any(), f32_finite(),\n    f32_mag(mag), f32_unit(), vec3_any(), vec3_finite(), vec3_mag(mag), vec4_any(),\n    vec3_any_1nan(), dir(), i32_any()\n\nC SIGNATURES you need (vec3_t == float[3] == *mut f32 / *const f32,\nvec4_t == float[4], `vec3_t x[3]` decays to `*mut [f32;3]`):\n  vec_t _DotProduct(const vec3_t v1, const vec3_t v2);\n  void _VectorSubtract(const vec3_t a, const vec3_t b, vec3_t out);\n  void _VectorAdd(const vec3_t a, const vec3_t b, vec3_t out);\n  void _VectorCopy(const vec3_t in, vec3_t out);\n  void _VectorScale(const vec3_t in, vec_t scale, vec3_t out);\n  void _VectorMA(const vec3_t veca, float scale, const vec3_t vecb, vec3_t vecc);\n  void Vector4Scale(const vec4_t in, vec_t scale, vec4_t out);\n  vec_t VectorNormalize(vec3_t v);          // in place, returns length\n  vec_t VectorNormalize2(const vec3_t v, vec3_t out);\n  float NormalizeColor(const vec3_t in, vec3_t out);\n  float RadiusFromBounds(const vec3_t mins, const vec3_t maxs);\n  void ClearBounds(vec3_t mins, vec3_t maxs);\n  void AddPointToBounds(const vec3_t v, vec3_t mins, vec3_t maxs);\n  void VectorRotate(vec3_t in, vec3_t matrix[3], vec3_t out);\n  void MatrixMultiply(float in1[3][3], float in2[3][3], float out[3][3]);\n\nTEST FUNCTIONS TO WRITE (names must be EXACTLY these, they are referenced by\nCONFIGS.md rows 18-31):\n  dot_product              (row 18: shapes S0/SA/SN/SR/SH/ST/SI/SNaN, 20000 random\n                            pairs, plus passing the SAME pointer as both args)\n  vector_add_sub           (row 19)\n  vector_ops_aliasing      (row 20: out aliases arg1, arg2, or both, for\n                            _VectorAdd/_VectorSubtract/_VectorScale/_VectorCopy/\n                            _VectorMA/Vector4Scale. Pass the same raw pointer for\n                            several params; the C code writes and reads in source\n                            order so results are well defined.)\n  vector_scale             (row 21)\n  vector_ma                (row 22)\n  vector_copy_bits         (row 23: _VectorCopy must copy every bit pattern\n                            verbatim, including arbitrary NaN payloads - use\n                            f32::from_bits(rng.next_u32()) and compare bits with\n                            assert_vec, NOT check_vec, since no arithmetic happens)\n  vector_normalize         (row 24: length!=0 / ==0 (zero vector, and {1e-30,0,0}\n                            whose square underflows) / NaN length; also verify the\n                            returned length AND the (possibly unmodified) vector)\n  vector_normalize2        (row 25: same length classes; separate output buffer AND\n                            the aliased out==v case)\n  normalize_color          (row 26)\n  radius_from_bounds       (row 27)\n  clear_bounds             (row 28: fresh buffers, and the aliased mins==maxs case)\n  add_point_to_bounds      (row 29: start from ClearBounds output, then points\n                            inside/outside on each of the 6 faces, NaN points,\n                            aliased mins==maxs)\n  vector_rotate            (row 30: identity/zero/random/singular matrices; also\n                            out==in aliasing)\n  matrix_multiply          (row 31: identity, zero, permutation, random, huge\n                            (overflow), NaN; aliasing out==in1, out==in2, in1==in2)\n\nNotes on faithful test construction:\n- For every function that writes through a pointer, initialise the C output\n  buffer and the Rust output buffer to the SAME garbage (e.g. copy the same\n  pre-filled array) so that \"not written\" is detected.\n- MatrixMultiply takes float[3][3]; declare `let mut m: [[f32;3];3]` and pass\n  `m.as_mut_ptr()` (type `*mut [f32;3]`).\n- Do not feed AngleSubtract-style huge values anywhere here; none of your\n  functions loop.\n\nWORKFLOW: write the file, then run\n  cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-MbXgVZ/translated_rust\n  ./build_c.sh >/dev/null && cargo build --offline 2>&1 | tail -3\n  timeout 600 cargo test --offline --test vectors 2>&1 | tail -40\nIterate until it compiles. Report: the final `cargo test` output summary, the\nlist of test fns you wrote, and any divergence you found (with exact inputs and\nbit patterns) WITHOUT fixing src/.","uuid":"f02a4aad-5369-4d4d-b56c-e813c0f7e150","session_id":"bf9d36cb-13a4-40a8-a93d-48ad7b006df9"}
