f64) / M_PI`, an f64 multiply with no intermediate rounding. For the failing input: `a*180.0f` = `0.70588237` (0x3f34b4b5) in C vs `0.70588239468634129` (0x3fe69696ad000000) in Rust.\n\nScope (measured with a standalone gcc program, same flags): the two spellings differ for **97 794 of 100 000** random finite f32 inputs, i.e. essentially every input whose f32 product is inexact. It is also an overflow difference, not just a rounding one: for `a = 1e38f` C yields `+inf` while the Rust version yields `5.72958e39`. `DEG2RAD` is *not* affected (`M_PI` is a `double`, so C already multiplies in f64) and passes over the whole pool.\n\nThe failing assert is the last block of `deg_rad_and_short_angles` on purpose (comment in the file), so `DEG2RAD`, `ANGLE2SHORT`, `SHORT2ANGLE` (incl. `INT_MIN..INT_MAX`) and `ColorIndex` are all fully verified before the panic.\n\n## One documented input-domain exclusion (not a weakened assertion)\n`ColorIndex(c)` = `((c) - '0') & 7`: for `c < i32::MIN + 48` the subtraction overflows — UB in C (gcc wraps) and an overflow **panic** in the debug-built Rust cdylib, which would abort the whole test process rather than report a mismatch. The sweep covers all 256 char values (both as unsigned byte and sign-extended), plenty of negatives, and `i32::MIN+48/+49/+100` (the smallest well-defined values), with the excluded range named in a comment. Worth noting as a translation fragility: the C wraps, the Rust `c - '0' as c_int` does not use `wrapping_sub` (unlike `Q_rand`, which does).\n\n## Notable behaviours confirmed identical (C == Rust, bit-exact)\n- `SnapVector`: matches an independent `cvttss2si` model; every out-of-range/NaN component becomes exactly `-2147483648.0f` = `0xcf000000` (ERRORS row 27), and `-2^31` produces the same float via a *valid* conversion.\n- `ANGLE2SHORT`: `|x| >= 11796480` (and NaN/±inf) → `0x80000000 & 65535 == 0` (ERRORS row 26); `-11796480.0` scales to exactly `-2^31`, a valid conversion that also yields 0.\n- `VectorCompare`: `+0.0 == -0.0` → 1; any NaN payload vs itself → 0.\n- `IS_NAN` reports ±inf as \"NaN\" too (exponent-only test), verified over all 256 exponents × 9 mantissa classes × both signs and 20 000 random words.\n- Macro forms vs `_`-prefixed function forms agree bit-for-bit on both sides (incl. `VectorMA`'s `b*s` vs `scale*b` operand order); sentinel slots prove no writer runs past the end of its vector.\n- `-x` in `VectorInverse`/`VectorNegate` is a pure sign flip: NaN payloads survive over 20 000 arbitrary bit patterns.\n- Layout: `sizeof(cplane_t)==20`, offsets `0,12,16,17,18`, `NUMVERTEXNORMALS==162`, `nanmask==0x7f800000`, `sizeof(vec_t)==4`, `PITCH/YAW/ROLL==0/1/2`, `PLANE_*==0..3`, `qfalse/qtrue==0/1`, `sizeof(qboolean)==4`, `w_M_PI() == std::f64::consts::PI` bit-for-bit (and not the f32 literal).\n- Data: all 15 symbols byte-identical (`vec3_origin`, `axisDefault`, 486 floats of `bytedirs` compared per-index, `g_color_table`, 11 colours); both `.so`s' `vec3_origin` is writable and read-back-identical (restored under a `Mutex` so the concurrent `#[test]` threads can't observe it); `ByteToDir(b)` for `b in 0..162` returns exactly `bytedirs[b]` of its *own* library on both sides."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":525,"cache_read_input_tokens":178124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":525,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01K5jGuJUfGMrBPtJB44XHbU","session_id":"bf9d36cb-13a4-40a8-a93d-48ad7b006df9","uuid":"f8aa90a6-d470-4094-91c6-341ba5b153f5","timestamp":"2026-08-20T14:00:40.534Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write q_shared wrapper + data tests"}
