{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0149pcEwGboBSCBaufRPnWaC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Differential tests for the angle / axis / vector-basis entry points of\n2\t//! `q_math.c` (CONFIGS.md rows 32-38 and 45-47):\n3\t//!\n4\t//! `AngleVectors` `AnglesToAxis` `AxisClear` `AxisCopy` `ProjectPointOnPlane`\n5\t//! `MakeNormalVectors` `PerpendicularVector` `vectoangles`\n6\t//! `RotatePointAroundVector` `RotateAroundDirection`.\n7\t//!\n8\t//! Every call goes through `dlsym` on both shared objects; every output byte is\n9\t//! compared with `f32::to_bits()`.  Output buffers are always pre-filled with\n10\t//! the SAME sentinel on both sides, so \"not written at all\" (a NULL output of\n11\t//! `AngleVectors`, the skipped rotation of `RotateAroundDirection`) is detected\n12\t//! as well as a wrong value.\n13\t//!\n14\t//! Two properties of this group of functions decide how they are compared:\n15\t//!\n16\t//! * They all manufacture NaNs internally -- `sin`/`cos` of a non-finite angle,\n17\t//!   `1.0f / 0.0f` for a zero `normal`/`src`/`dir`, `0 * inf` -- and the source\n18\t//!   itself negates intermediates (`right[1] = -forward[0]`, `-1*sr*sp*cy`,\n19\t//!   `-d`, `-sy`), which flips the sign bit of a NaN.  Two different NaN\n20\t//!   patterns therefore meet inside single expressions such as\n21\t//!   `right[2]*forward[0] - right[0]*forward[2]`, where the surviving payload is\n22\t//!   whatever operand order the compiler picked for `mulss`/`addss`.  Results\n23\t//!   are therefore compared with `check_vec`, which tolerates a NaN payload\n24\t//!   difference only when both sides produced a NaN *and* an input was\n25\t//!   non-finite; NaN-ness, every finite value, every signed zero and every\n26\t//!   infinity are still compared bit for bit.  For all-finite inputs the\n27\t//!   comparison is fully exact (`assert_vec` semantics), and every routine here\n28\t//!   is NaN-free for finite inputs.\n29\t//! * `Angl