; 0 mismatches where either side was not NaN; 0 mismatches of any kind for all-finite inputs.**\n\n1. `AngleVectors` (propagates into `AnglesToAxis`) — gcc constant-folds the source's `-1*sr` into a sign-bit `xorps` and folds `-1*cr*-sy` into `cr*sy`; the Rust translation emits real `-1.0 *` multiplies, and `mulss` *preserves* a NaN's sign whereas `xorps` flips it. Exact bit patterns:\n   - `angles=[0,0,inf]`: C `right=[ffc00000,7fc00000,7fc00000] up=[ffc00000,7fc00000,ffc00000]` vs Rust `right=[ffc00000,ffc00000,ffc00000] up=[ffc00000,ffc00000,ffc00000]`\n   - `angles=[0,0,NaN]`: C `right=[7fc00000,ffc00000,ffc00000] up=[7fc00000,ffc00000,7fc00000]` vs Rust all `7fc00000`\n   - `AnglesToAxis([0,0,inf])`: `axis[1][1]` C `0x7fc00000` vs Rust `0xffc00000`\n   Reproducing gcc would mean writing `-sr * sp * cy` and `cr * sy` in `src/q_math.rs`. I did not do that.\n2. `MakeNormalVectors(forward=[NaN,1,0])`: `up[1]` C `0x7fc00000` vs Rust `0xffc00000` — the source's own `right[1] = -forward[0]` makes `-NaN` meet the caller's `+NaN` in `right[2]*forward[0] - right[0]*forward[2]`; the survivor is decided by `mulss` operand order (gcc register allocation), not by the source.\n3. `ProjectPointOnPlane(p=[NaN,1,2], normal=[0,0,0])`: `dst[1]` C `0xffc00000` vs Rust `0x7fc00000` — the internal `1.0f/0.0f` makes `+inf`, `0*inf` makes the x86 default `-NaN`, which meets the caller's `+NaN`.\n\nAll three are inside the suite's documented NaN-payload policy, so the tests pass rather than fail: `check_vec` tolerates a payload difference only when both sides are NaN *and* an input was non-finite. That policy is exactly what the harness was broadened to express while I was working (`nan_payload_ambiguous` now = \"any non-finite or overflowing input\"), so no artificial input tainting was needed in my file — every finite case is compared fully bit-exactly.\n\n## One more real finding, worth recording\n\nC `AngleVectors` is **not reentrant**: `sr, sp, sy, cr, cp, cy` are file-scope `static float`s (visible in the disassembly as globals `sy.5`, `cy.4`, `sp.3`, `cp.2`, `sr.1`, `cr.0`), while the Rust translation uses locals. Under libtest's parallel threads the C side genuinely corrupts itself — e.g. `angles=[90,0,0]`, forward-only: C returned `[0.70710677, -1.2363448e-7, -0.70710677]` (another thread's sines/cosines) vs Rust's correct `[-4.371139e-8, -0.0, -1.0]`. My two AngleVectors-reaching tests therefore take `harness::angle_vectors_guard()`. That guard is a new harness API added concurrently by another agent — if it were removed, `tests/angles.rs` would stop compiling."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2474,"cache_read_input_tokens":164466,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2474,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DyAy6ZjHwM1cxNNX7xT5F5","session_id":"bf9d36cb-13a4-40a8-a93d-48ad7b006df9","uuid":"be8b15e8-3020-4bdd-ab76-2d07dc7f7993","timestamp":"2026-08-20T14:02:20.761Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write angle/matrix differential tests"}
